# Compose-level configuration for the celld stack: hostnames, buckets, and the
# credentials celld itself uses to reach its fleet bucket.
#
#   cp .env.example .env    # then edit
#
# This file configures DOCKER. Application config and secrets go in the other
# file — celld-vars.env — which is delivered to the Worker at runtime. Two
# values are deliberately duplicated across both; see "the duplicated values"
# at the bottom, because getting them out of step fails in a confusing way.

# --- hostnames --------------------------------------------------------------
# *_SITE carries a SCHEME and is the Caddy site address. The scheme is what
# decides whether Caddy runs auto-HTTPS:
#
#   http://app.localhost   plain HTTP, no certificates, no ACME  (local)
#   nadi.example.com       Caddy issues a Let's Encrypt cert     (server)
#
# For a real server, drop the `http://` from all three and point DNS at the
# machine BEFORE first start — Caddy issues on startup and Let's Encrypt
# rate-limits failures.
#
# Local note: the app resolves artifact origins as http:// only for `localhost`
# and `*.localhost` hosts, and https:// for everything else
# (src/http/artifact-routes.ts). So a local setup must use `.localhost` names —
# `nadi.test` or `nadi.local` would mint https:// artifact URLs that nothing
# serves.
NADI_SITE=http://app.localhost
ARTIFACTS_SITE=http://artifacts.localhost
S3_SITE=http://s3.localhost

# The bare hostname from S3_SITE, no scheme. Attached to the Caddy container as
# a Docker network alias so that ONE endpoint value works from both sides:
# the browser (127.0.0.1 via /etc/hosts) and the Worker inside the container
# (Docker DNS -> Caddy -> MinIO). It has to be one value, because SigV4 signs
# the Host header — a presigned URL made with a different hostname than the one
# the request arrives with fails the signature check.
S3_HOSTNAME=s3.localhost

# Only used when a *_SITE has no scheme (i.e. Caddy is issuing certificates).
# Harmless but required otherwise: an empty value makes Caddy's global `email`
# directive fail to parse and the container will not start.
ACME_EMAIL=you@example.com

# --- buckets ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Two names for one bucket, and they must agree: `celld` wants an s3:// URL,
# while the MinIO bootstrap wants a bare name to create.
CELLD_BUCKET=s3://celld-fleet
CELLD_FLEET_BUCKET=celld-fleet

# Created by the `minio` profile. Also set in celld-vars.env — see below.
S3_ATTACHMENTS_BUCKET_NAME=nadi-attachments

# --- S3 credentials ---------------------------------------------------------
# These are what CELLD uses for the fleet bucket, and with the bundled `minio`
# profile they are also MinIO's root credentials, so they are created rather
# than looked up. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('hex'))"
# With an external S3/R2 bucket, put that provider's keys here instead.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# How celld and the deploy job reach S3 from INSIDE the compose network. This
# one is never seen by a browser, so a Docker service name is correct here —
# unlike S3_ENDPOINT in celld-vars.env, which is browser-facing.
# Point at an external endpoint instead if you are not using the minio profile.
S3_ENDPOINT_INTERNAL=http://minio:9000

# --- celld durability -------------------------------------------------------
# How soon a quiet cell replicates to the bucket. This is the ONLY thing
# bounding what an unclean stop costs, and it must stay well under the
# scheduler's 60s tick — past ~60s the registry never idles, never replicates,
# and a crash loses everything back to the last restart.
#
# 35 rather than a smaller number because of one specific interaction: the SPA
# sends a presence heartbeat every 30s (`setInterval(sendPresence, 30_000)` in
# web/src/App.tsx). Any value BELOW 30 is the worst of both worlds — the
# heartbeat is too slow to keep the UserHub cell warm, but reliably drags it
# back for a ~190ms cold start, forever, once per open tab. Measured at 15:
# UserHub cold-started every 30.0s to the millisecond.
#
# Going above the heartbeat costs nothing in durability for that cell, because
# UserHub stores nothing — it holds live sockets only (src/agent/user-hub.ts),
# with presence on the socket's serializeAttachment. There is no state of its
# own to replicate.
#
# It DOES widen the window for cells that do hold state (RegistryDatabase,
# ThinkThreadAgent): an unclean stop now costs up to ~35s of writes instead of
# ~15s. Lower it if that trade is wrong for you; the floor for avoiding the
# treadmill is 31, and the ceiling is dictated by the 60s tick — keep at least
# ~20s of margin, so do not exceed ~40.
CELLD_IDLE_EVICT_S=35

# Lets the ticker's cell evict between ticks so its alarm is persisted. Without
# it the always-imminent 60s alarm keeps the cell resident forever, so it never
# replicates and scheduled work silently stops after a restart.
CELLD_ALARM_RESIDENT_MS=1000

# --- the duplicated values --------------------------------------------------
# Kept in step by hand across this file and celld-vars.env:
#
#   S3_ATTACHMENTS_BUCKET_NAME   here it is the bucket MinIO CREATES;
#                                there it is the bucket the Worker WRITES TO.
#                                Different values means uploads 404 against a
#                                bucket that was never made.
#
#   S3_HOSTNAME / S3_ENDPOINT    here, bare, for the Docker network alias;
#                                there, as a full URL the browser can open.
#                                They must name the same host.
